Evidence checklist
- [ ] Identify source process and target process/PID.
- [ ] Record process handle and requested access rights.
- [ ] Record remote allocation base, size and protection.
- [ ] Verify each memory write falls inside the tracked allocation.
- [ ] Inspect source bytes and distinguish a DLL path from executable payload bytes.
- [ ] Correlate thread start address and parameter with the allocated memory.
- [ ] Record return values and error codes; a breakpoint hit is not proof of success.
- [ ] Check Process Hacker memory regions and Procmon file/registry activity.
- [ ] Investigate anti-debugging and environment checks if execution differs under a debugger.
- [ ] Capture debugger screenshots and note the VM snapshot used.
x86 argument reminder
At a typical 32-bit stdcall API entry, [ESP] is the return address, [ESP+4] is the first argument, [ESP+8] the second, and so on. Examine EAX after the call for a return value when appropriate. On x64 Windows, the first four integer/pointer arguments are normally in RCX, RDX, R8, and R9.
Caveats
- CreateProcessA/W, LoadLibraryA/W, and other exports may forward into KernelBase.dll on Windows 10.
- Native Nt* functions are usually in ntdll.dll.
- RtlCopyMemory and similar routines may be macros/inlined, so an exported symbol breakpoint may not exist.
- Some APIs are not available on every Windows version or may not yet be loaded into the debuggee.
- A memory region marked RWX is an investigative lead, not proof of malicious injection.
- Use only in an isolated, authorized analysis environment.
